      "text": "# Review: R4 Starvation in the Central Relay Scheduler\n\n## Bottom line\n\n- **R4 = 0 does not by itself show a throughput defect.** The 9/8/3/0 and 10/10/4/0 splits are what a first-available upstream cascade produces when the feeder rarely keeps three robots busy at once.\n- **The scheduler already falls through to R4 under saturation.** When R1–R3 are busy they are excluded from bidding. A box that reaches R4's window then has R4 as its only eligible bidder: upstream robots it has passed wrap to more than 20 s away and are dropped. R4 only loses when an upstream robot is idle and willing.\n- **Replacing `10*used` with a cumulative-work regularizer is not defensible as a throughput fix.** It is defensible only as an explicit fairness or wear-leveling objective, gated to low demand, and tested for its cost.\n- **There are two real issues worth fixing first, both independent of fairness:**\n  1. Multiple boxes can be softly deferred to the same downstream robot.\n  2. `futureFitRatio` is conditioned on each robot's own feasible sample, which flatters fuller pallets.\n\n## How R4 loses today\n\nPer-bid score: `45·fit + 25·(1−height) + 20/(1+(arrival+work)/10) + 10·used`.\n\n| Term | Effect on R4 (empty pallet, downstream) | Typical magnitude |\n|---|---|---|\n| Time term | Upstream eligible bids have `arrival=0`. R4 is always the latest arrival among competitors. | With work = 8 s: 11.1 (a=0) vs 8.7 (a=5), 7.1 (a=10), 5.3 (a=20). **R4 penalty about 2.4–5.8.** |\n| `10·used` | R4 has used = 0. R1 with 9 boxes has perhaps 0.2–0.4. | **R4 penalty about 2–4.** Deliberate consolidation pressure. |\n| Height | R4's floor placement is low. Non-empty cells prefer elevated sites for small parcels (the −100 floor penalty plus `slice(0,2)`). | Usually **favors R4**, by several points. |\n| Fit | Quantized in steps of 1/3 (or 1/2, or 1). Often 1.0 for everyone early on. | Dominates whenever it differs (±15–45). |\n| Tie rule | `b.score >= future` lets an upstream bid win ties. | Small but systematic. |\n| Horizon | `arrivalSeconds > 20` drops R4's bid entirely for boxes still far upstream. | R4 may be absent rather than losing. |\n\nSo R4 needs about 5–10 points of fit/height advantage to beat an idle upstream robot. Without a per-term breakdown, you cannot tell whether R4 lost on time, on `used`, on a tie, or never bid. **Instrument before changing weights.**\n\n## Concrete findings\n\n### F1. Multiple boxes can be deferred to the same downstream robot (correctness/throughput risk, exists today)\n\nIn `rankAssignments`, `future` is computed per offer, independently. The DP mask only tracks launching robots.\n\n- If two boxes both have R4 as their best ineligible bid, both have their upstream eligible bids filtered out and both become soft intentions to R4.\n- R4 can take one. The other passes R4's window, gets `passes ≥ 1`, and recirculates a full loop.\n\nA fairness term that raises R4's scores makes this much more frequent.\n\n### F2. Soft intentions are unreserved, so every deferral is a bet\n\n`launchNow:false` reserves nothing. If R4 is busy, swaps pallets, changes cell version, or fails `interceptAction` when the box arrives, the box recirculates. Note that `interceptAction` rejects grasps past the 1050 window edge or off the straight run, so R4's position near the end of the straight matters. The upstream robot that declined the box stayed idle for nothing.\n\n**Any change that makes R4 win more often does so by increasing deferrals. It does not reassign boxes that are already eligible.**\n\n### F3. `futureFitRatio` is conditioned on each robot's own feasible sample\n\n`before` is the subset of the three largest observed boxes that currently fit this robot. `fit = retained / before.length`, and an empty `before` gives `fit = 1`.\n\n- A nearly full R1 that can no longer take any of the large boxes still scores fit = 1.\n- An empty R4 is judged against all three.\n\nThis systematically flatters fuller pallets in cross-robot comparisons, and it is a pallet-use signal, not a fairness one. A cross-robot option-value measure fixes it: count observed boxes that still have at least one feasible cell after the placement. That is closer to what `planIndependentStream`'s `elsewhere` check does.\n\n### F4. The 20 s horizon makes R4 structurally absent for boxes at R1\n\nCheck `stationArc` spacing against belt speed. If R1→R4 transit exceeds 20 s, R1 never sees R4 as a competitor. Log \"R4 excluded by horizon\" separately from \"R4 bid and lost.\"\n\n### F5. Busy robots are invisible, not merely penalized\n\nA robot finishing in 1 s is treated the same as one finishing in 30 s. This is the real load-balancing lever under pressure, and the proposed regularizer does not touch it.\n\n### F6. Single seed, counts only\n\nThe evidence is one seed and placement counts. There is no makespan, belt dwell, recirculation count, or idle-while-stock-passed metric yet, so starvation-as-harm is unproven.\n\n## Assessment of the proposed regularizer\n\nThe proposal replaces `10·used` with a bounded cumulative completed-work term.\n\n**Where it is defensible.** If the operational goal is wear leveling, or balanced per-pallet output, and that goal is stated explicitly.\n\n**Where it is not.** As a throughput or makespan fix, for these reasons:\n\n- **Sign of benefit under saturation.** When demand saturates R1–R3, R4 already gets boxes through the fall-through path. When it doesn't, R4 idling costs nothing. The only way the regularizer changes outcomes is by causing more deferrals:\n  - Under pressure, deferrals are harmful: an idle upstream robot is passed over, the box dwells longer, and F1/F2 failures lead to recirculation.\n  - Under low demand, deferrals are roughly neutral for makespan but add belt dwell.\n- **It is history, not state.** Cumulative work does not tell you who is free, who has capacity, or who finishes soonest. It also needs a defined reset at pallet swap (`cycle`), or it penalizes a robot for a previous pallet.\n- **Pallet-use risk.** Removing `10·used` removes the only consolidation incentive. Spreading 20 boxes over four pallets leaves four partial pallets instead of two or three fuller ones. Expect:\n  - lower end-of-run fill density,\n  - later pallet completion,\n  - possibly more fragmented floors, though the effect on large late arrivals is mixed and must be measured.\n- **Interaction with `future`.** A bounded term of about 10 points is as large as the entire time term. It will move the `b.score >= future` threshold often, not just break ties.\n\n## Fair utilization vs. makespan\n\n| | Fair utilization | Makespan / throughput |\n|---|---|---|\n| What it measures | Spread of work or boxes across R1–R4 | Time to last placement; boxes per hour; belt dwell; recirculations |\n| When R4 idle matters | Always (by definition) | Only if a box recirculated or waited while R4 was loading and idle, or upstream busy time is the bottleneck |\n| Correct lever | Explicit low-demand preference with a bounded cost | Availability-aware assignment (projected free time of busy robots); fix F1/F2 |\n| Cost | Extra dwell, more soft-intention misses, lower consolidation | — |\n\n**Do not use utilization balance as a proxy for makespan.** In a serial belt, upstream-first is near-optimal for latency whenever capacity is slack.\n\n## Recommended minimal, testable path\n\n### Step 0: Instrument (no behavior change)\n\n- Log per bid: `{fit, heightTerm, timeTerm, usedTerm, arrival, before.length}`.\n- Per offer, log the winner's margin over R4 and the reason R4 was absent (`horizon`, `busy`, `phase`, `no-candidate`).\n\nRun counters:\n\n- recirculations (`passes ≥ 1`)\n- soft intentions issued vs. honored\n- mean and p95 belt dwell (measured → grasp)\n- makespan\n- per-robot busy fraction\n- maximum concurrent busy count among R1–R3\n- end-of-run fill ratio per pallet\n- **R4 idle while a box it could fit passed or recirculated** (the actual starvation-harm metric)\n\n### Step 1: Fix F1 (correctness, any objective)\n\nAllow at most one outstanding soft intention per downstream robot per tick. Compute `future` only over robots not already softly claimed in this pass. Process offers in the existing order and mark the claimed robot.\n\n```ts\nconst softClaimed=new Set<number>();\n// per offer:\nconst fut=offer.bids.filter(b=>!b.eligible&&!softClaimed.has(b.robot));\nconst future=Math.max(-Infinity,...fut.map(b=>b.score));\n// after selection, if the winner is a soft intention: softClaimed.add(winner.robot)\n```\n\n**Acceptance:** recirculations and broken soft intentions do not increase; makespan does not worsen across a seed sweep.\n\n### Step 2: Run a demand sweep before any weight change\n\nUse 20+ seeds, box counts of 20/24/40, and feeder rates from the current rate up to saturation (R1–R3 concurrently busy).\n\nIf R4 stays near zero only at low rates and rises under stress with no recirculation or dwell penalty, **close the issue as expected behavior.**\n\n### Step 3: Only if an explicit fairness objective exists\n\nKeep `10·used`. Add a tie-break-sized term (λ ≤ 2–3, smaller than the time-term gap) based on completed work in the current cycle, normalized. Apply it only when **all** of these hold:\n\n- demand pressure = 0 (no box with `passes ≥ 1`, and observed in-horizon boxes ≤ idle loading robots),\n- the target robot has no outstanding soft intention,\n- `arrival ≤` a tighter bound (for example, 10 s).\n\nReport its cost explicitly as Δdwell, Δsoft-intention misses, and Δend fill ratio versus Step 1. Never apply it under pressure.\n\n### Optional follow-up for pallet use\n\nReplace self-conditioned `fit` (F3) with a cross-robot option-value measure. Evaluate it on end-of-run fill and on large-box recirculations, not on R4's count.\n\nThis sequence never forces R4 busy when the feeder lacks demand. It also separates the throughput question (Steps 0–2) from the fairness preference (Step 3), so each can be accepted or rejected on its own metric."
